1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,025
Haw Par Villa: The Tiger
Balm Garden of Statues

2
00:00:04,025 --> 00:00:07,809
On a hill above Pasir Panjang
Road stands the strangest

3
00:00:07,889 --> 00:00:12,095
garden in Singapore: a terraced
hillside crowded with painted

4
00:00:12,175 --> 00:00:16,100
concrete statues — gods and
demons, monkeys and mermaids,

5
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:20,315
sinners being sawn in half —
built by the Tiger Balm fortune

6
00:00:20,395 --> 00:00:24,400
as part gift, part advertisement
and part moral textbook.

7
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:28,748
It has outlived the mansion it was
built around, the company town of

8
00:00:28,828 --> 00:00:33,125
theme parks that tried to replace
it, and the family that made it.

9
00:00:33,125 --> 00:00:35,839
Today it waits behind a partial closure

10
00:00:35,919 --> 00:00:39,000
while the state decides
what to do with it.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,148
This is the garden's own story;
the brothers who paid for it,

12
00:00:43,228 --> 00:00:47,525
Aw Boon Haw and Aw Boon Par,
have their own post on this blog.

13
00:00:47,525 --> 00:00:49,770
The founding can be told briefly,

14
00:00:49,850 --> 00:00:52,950
because the brothers'
post tells it in full.

15
00:00:52,950 --> 00:00:58,591
In 1935 Aw Boon Haw bought a
seafront hill on Pasir Panjang Road,

16
00:00:58,671 --> 00:01:01,575
on the rural south-western coast.

17
00:01:01,575 --> 00:01:06,006
There the architect Ho Kwang
Yew built an Art Deco villa,

18
00:01:06,086 --> 00:01:11,625
finished in 1937, which Boon Haw
gave to his younger brother Boon Par.

19
00:01:11,625 --> 00:01:15,070
The name Haw Par joins
the brothers' two names;

20
00:01:15,150 --> 00:01:18,750
in Chinese the place is
the Tiger Leopard Villa.

21
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:22,814
It was the second Tiger Balm
Garden — Boon Haw had built

22
00:01:22,894 --> 00:01:27,771
the first behind a house in Hong
Kong in 1932 — and like the first,

23
00:01:27,851 --> 00:01:31,625
its garden was opened to
the public free of charge.

24
00:01:31,625 --> 00:01:34,675
Boon Haw made no secret of why.

25
00:01:34,675 --> 00:01:38,298
The National Library Board
records that he wanted the

26
00:01:38,378 --> 00:01:42,150
garden to be an advertisement
for Tiger Balm products.

27
00:01:42,150 --> 00:01:46,788
It was also, quite deliberately,
a school: its statues were meant

28
00:01:46,868 --> 00:01:51,579
to teach Chinese cultural values
— filial piety, charity, loyalty,

29
00:01:51,659 --> 00:01:56,450
resistance to temptation, and what
awaited the wicked after death.

30
00:01:56,450 --> 00:02:00,120
The Singapore garden climbed
its hill in four terraces,

31
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:04,700
where the Hong Kong garden had
been built up a single steep plane.

32
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:08,250
At ground level came
the garage and parking.

33
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:12,133
The second tier held a large
fish pond that served as

34
00:02:12,213 --> 00:02:16,325
the family's aquarium, stocked
with fish and tortoises.

35
00:02:16,325 --> 00:02:19,658
The third was the family's
own pleasure ground —

36
00:02:19,738 --> 00:02:22,725
a lawn, tennis courts and a swimming pool.

37
00:02:22,725 --> 00:02:26,432
On the fourth and uppermost
tier stood the villa itself,

38
00:02:26,512 --> 00:02:30,975
with its gold-gilded domes, looking
out over the Singapore Strait.

39
00:02:30,975 --> 00:02:35,827
The garden of 1937 was, by the
National Library Board's account,

40
00:02:35,907 --> 00:02:39,758
a simple one — some rock
structures among gateways,

41
00:02:39,838 --> 00:02:43,075
ponds and pavilions in the Chinese manner.

42
00:02:43,075 --> 00:02:46,955
There was even a small zoo at
first: animals roamed freely,

43
00:02:47,035 --> 00:02:50,646
were later caged, and were
finally removed altogether —

44
00:02:50,726 --> 00:02:52,928
snakes were said to have escaped —

45
00:02:53,008 --> 00:02:56,900
and replaced by animal statues,
which were easier to keep.

46
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:00,230
The pagoda that still
rises over the grounds was

47
00:03:00,310 --> 00:03:04,136
originally fifteen storeys
tall; Boon Haw later had its

48
00:03:04,216 --> 00:03:08,612
top storeys taken down and replaced
with a statue of Sakyamuni,

49
00:03:08,692 --> 00:03:11,675
leaving the four storeys that stand today.

50
00:03:11,675 --> 00:03:15,367
The statues were made by
craftsmen brought from Swatow,

51
00:03:15,447 --> 00:03:19,150
in Guangdong, for their
knowledge of Chinese folklore.

52
00:03:19,150 --> 00:03:22,536
The National Library Board
names the first of them:

53
00:03:22,616 --> 00:03:26,953
Kwek Hoon Sua, who had worked on
the Hong Kong garden and brought

54
00:03:27,033 --> 00:03:30,975
over his brother Kwek Choon
Sua and a team of apprentices.

55
00:03:30,975 --> 00:03:34,766
The Singapore team also
included a father and son,

56
00:03:34,846 --> 00:03:38,175
Tan See Hua and Tan
Seng Hua, and a Mr Liu.

57
00:03:38,175 --> 00:03:40,175
There were no blueprints.

58
00:03:40,175 --> 00:03:44,059
Boon Haw directed the work in
person, walking the grounds in

59
00:03:44,139 --> 00:03:48,750
early-morning rounds of about two
hours on his visits every few months,

60
00:03:48,830 --> 00:03:52,781
inspecting what the sculptors
had made and ordering changes —

61
00:03:52,861 --> 00:03:56,825
down, the National Library Board
notes, to the colours used.

62
00:03:56,825 --> 00:04:00,098
The garden was in that
sense drawn at full scale,

63
00:04:00,178 --> 00:04:03,931
in concrete, by a man selling
ointment: every tableau an

64
00:04:04,011 --> 00:04:09,474
advertisement's argument that this was a
family firm rooted in Chinese tradition,

65
00:04:09,554 --> 00:04:11,938
and every moral a lesson the visitor

66
00:04:12,018 --> 00:04:15,850
was meant to carry home along
with the name of the balm.

67
00:04:15,850 --> 00:04:17,798
The exhibits mixed Buddhist,

68
00:04:17,878 --> 00:04:22,225
Taoist and Confucian material
with Chinese history and myth.

69
00:04:22,225 --> 00:04:25,445
There were the Virtues and Vices tableaux,

70
00:04:25,525 --> 00:04:28,825
a lane of moral tales in painted concrete.

71
00:04:28,825 --> 00:04:32,378
There was Journey to the
West, the Monkey King's story;

72
00:04:32,458 --> 00:04:36,025
the Eight Immortals Crossing
the Sea; Monkey Mountain.

73
00:04:36,025 --> 00:04:39,905
Sculpted plants and animals
carried their own meanings —

74
00:04:39,985 --> 00:04:43,025
the lotus for purity,
the carp for success.

75
00:04:43,025 --> 00:04:46,474
The point of it all was
instruction by spectacle:

76
00:04:46,554 --> 00:04:50,148
virtue and vice set side
by side, larger than life,

77
00:04:50,228 --> 00:04:53,325
in a garden anyone
could walk through free.

78
00:04:53,325 --> 00:04:57,450
Boon Par enjoyed his villa
for only a few years.

79
00:04:57,450 --> 00:05:01,662
On the eve of the Japanese
Occupation in 1942

80
00:05:01,742 --> 00:05:06,225
he left for Rangoon, and
he died there in 1944.

81
00:05:06,225 --> 00:05:09,921
The compound suffered extensive
damage during the war —

82
00:05:10,001 --> 00:05:15,150
the villa was bombed — and after the
war the ruined mansion was demolished.

83
00:05:15,150 --> 00:05:18,299
No official account gives
the demolition a date,

84
00:05:18,379 --> 00:05:21,192
and nothing was built
in the house's place;

85
00:05:21,272 --> 00:05:24,300
the hilltop simply became
part of the garden.

86
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:26,958
What Boon Haw did next is the making

87
00:05:27,038 --> 00:05:29,700
of the place as Singapore knows it.

88
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:33,028
Returning from Hong Kong,
he rebuilt the garden and

89
00:05:33,108 --> 00:05:36,503
greatly enlarged it, adding
sculptures in the spaces

90
00:05:36,583 --> 00:05:39,578
that had been the family's
recreation ground —

91
00:05:39,658 --> 00:05:43,400
over the swimming pool, the
tennis courts and the lawns.

92
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:48,150
Most of the famous statuary dates
from this post-war rebuilding.

93
00:05:48,150 --> 00:05:51,675
So, above all, do the Ten Courts of Hell.

94
00:05:51,675 --> 00:05:55,433
The Courts are a walk-through
of the underworld of Chinese

95
00:05:55,513 --> 00:05:59,733
Buddhist and Taoist belief, in
which the dead are judged court by

96
00:05:59,813 --> 00:06:03,902
court by ten Yama kings, each
presiding over punishments fitted

97
00:06:03,982 --> 00:06:08,150
to particular sins, before their
next reincarnation is decided.

98
00:06:08,150 --> 00:06:11,575
The idea underneath the
gore is cause and effect —

99
00:06:11,655 --> 00:06:15,010
one reaps what one sows
— and the garden's makers

100
00:06:15,090 --> 00:06:18,525
meant it as the moral core
of the whole hillside.

101
00:06:18,525 --> 00:06:21,975
The entrance is guarded
by Ox-Head and Horse-Face,

102
00:06:22,055 --> 00:06:23,750
the escorts of the dead.

103
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:28,550
In the First Court, King Qinguang
conducts the preliminary trial.

104
00:06:28,550 --> 00:06:31,765
The virtuous cross the
Golden Bridge to paradise;

105
00:06:31,845 --> 00:06:36,271
those whose good deeds outweigh
their sins cross the Silver Bridge;

106
00:06:36,351 --> 00:06:39,163
the wicked are shown
their own faces in the

107
00:06:39,243 --> 00:06:42,875
Mirror of Retribution and
sent on to the courts below.

108
00:06:42,875 --> 00:06:47,550
The punishments, set out on the
courts' own signage, are specific.

109
00:06:47,550 --> 00:06:50,750
In the Second Court, those
who inflicted injury,

110
00:06:50,830 --> 00:06:54,508
and conmen and robbers, are
thrown into a volcanic pit;

111
00:06:54,588 --> 00:06:58,825
the corrupt, thieves and gamblers
are frozen in blocks of ice.

112
00:06:58,825 --> 00:07:02,777
In the Third Court, the ungrateful
and those who disrespected

113
00:07:02,857 --> 00:07:07,138
their elders have their hearts cut
out, while drug traffickers and

114
00:07:07,218 --> 00:07:11,250
tomb robbers are tied to a
red-hot copper pillar and grilled.

115
00:07:11,250 --> 00:07:14,643
In the Fifth Court, those
who plotted another's death

116
00:07:14,723 --> 00:07:19,033
for his property, and moneylenders
who charged exorbitant interest,

117
00:07:19,113 --> 00:07:21,275
are thrown onto a hill of knives.

118
00:07:21,275 --> 00:07:24,718
Generations of Singaporean
schoolchildren were walked

119
00:07:24,798 --> 00:07:28,839
through the Courts on school
trips and came out the other side

120
00:07:28,919 --> 00:07:32,575
promising to be good — which
was, of course, the point.

121
00:07:32,575 --> 00:07:36,339
The Courts' graphic reputation
only grew with retelling,

122
00:07:36,419 --> 00:07:40,389
and the garden collected the
usual folklore of such places,

123
00:07:40,469 --> 00:07:44,450
including the legend that the
statues come alive at night.

124
00:07:44,450 --> 00:07:47,633
Boon Haw died in 1954, and the garden

125
00:07:47,713 --> 00:07:50,800
passed to his nephew Aw Cheng Chye.

126
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:53,711
Cheng Chye added the
International Corners —

127
00:07:53,791 --> 00:07:59,014
sculptures representing the cultures of
the world, among them a sumo wrestler,

128
00:07:59,094 --> 00:08:04,248
a Thai dancer and a replica of the Statue
of Liberty — together with replicas

129
00:08:04,328 --> 00:08:08,475
of Tiger Brand products, the
family's goods joining the gods.

130
00:08:08,475 --> 00:08:12,771
After his death in 1971 the
family made no major changes,

131
00:08:12,851 --> 00:08:19,450
and the garden settled into its long peak as
one of Singapore's great free attractions:

132
00:08:19,530 --> 00:08:22,982
in the 1970s and 1980s
it drew, by the Straits

133
00:08:23,062 --> 00:08:27,975
Times figure commonly cited, at
least a million visitors a year.

134
00:08:27,975 --> 00:08:30,851
In 1985 the government acquired the

135
00:08:30,931 --> 00:08:34,225
grounds under the Land Acquisition Act.

136
00:08:34,225 --> 00:08:37,667
The Aw family donated the
statues to the nation,

137
00:08:37,747 --> 00:08:40,823
on one condition: that
the Haw Par name and

138
00:08:40,903 --> 00:08:44,425
the family memorials in
the grounds be retained.

139
00:08:44,425 --> 00:08:48,975
The Singapore Tourist Promotion
Board took over the management,

140
00:08:49,055 --> 00:08:51,180
and in 1988 leased the park to

141
00:08:51,260 --> 00:08:55,450
International Theme Parks Ltd
to develop as a theme park.

142
00:08:55,450 --> 00:09:00,054
The result, reopened in 1990,
was Haw Par Villa Dragon World —

143
00:09:00,134 --> 00:09:04,888
an 80 Singapore dollars-million
theme park with an amphitheatre,

144
00:09:04,968 --> 00:09:08,439
theatres, and the Ten
Courts of Hell rebuilt as

145
00:09:08,519 --> 00:09:12,825
a boat ride through the inside
of a 60-metre-long dragon.

146
00:09:12,825 --> 00:09:16,650
It charged admission, and
it lost money heavily.

147
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:19,538
In 2001 the operator gave up its rights,

148
00:09:19,618 --> 00:09:24,064
the board took the park back,
and Haw Par Villa became a free

149
00:09:24,144 --> 00:09:28,150
public park again, as it had
been in the family's day.

150
00:09:28,150 --> 00:09:29,964
The dragon was demolished,

151
00:09:30,044 --> 00:09:33,250
and the Courts went
back behind stone walls.

152
00:09:33,250 --> 00:09:37,419
A museum of the Chinese
diaspora, the Hua Song Museum,

153
00:09:37,499 --> 00:09:43,084
occupied part of the park from 2005
until it closed in 2012; in 2014 the

154
00:09:43,164 --> 00:09:48,434
tourist board marked its own fiftieth
anniversary with a festival in

155
00:09:48,514 --> 00:09:53,550
the grounds that drew more than
12,000 people over two weekends.

156
00:09:53,550 --> 00:09:58,403
In 2015 the tourist board
appointed Journeys Private Limited,

157
00:09:58,483 --> 00:10:01,475
a heritage operator, to run the park.

158
00:10:01,475 --> 00:10:05,464
Journeys closed it in October
2020 for extensive works —

159
00:10:05,544 --> 00:10:09,752
conservation of the sculptures
including the entrance arch,

160
00:10:09,832 --> 00:10:15,202
new night lighting, and the Ten Courts
of Hell made fully air-conditioned —

161
00:10:15,282 --> 00:10:18,625
and the park reopened
on the 1st of July 2021.

162
00:10:18,625 --> 00:10:22,532
That October came the boldest
addition since Boon Haw's day:

163
00:10:22,612 --> 00:10:26,651
Hell's Museum, billed as the
world's first museum dedicated to

164
00:10:26,731 --> 00:10:30,903
death and the afterlife — 3,800
square metres on how the world's

165
00:10:30,983 --> 00:10:35,023
religions and cultures treat
death, with replicas of an altar,

166
00:10:35,103 --> 00:10:39,541
a burial crypt, a traditional Chinese
grave and a void-deck funeral,

167
00:10:39,621 --> 00:10:43,275
wrapped around the Ten Courts,
which now sit inside it.

168
00:10:43,275 --> 00:10:46,778
The park had by then acquired
one more distinction:

169
00:10:46,858 --> 00:10:52,470
the Circle Line station beside its gate,
opened in 2011, is named Haw Par Villa —

170
00:10:52,550 --> 00:10:56,625
the garden had become a place
on the map in its own right.

171
00:10:56,625 --> 00:10:59,175
And there the story pauses.

172
00:10:59,175 --> 00:11:02,463
Journeys' contract ended on
the 7th of December 2025,

173
00:11:02,543 --> 00:11:07,294
and from the next day the Singapore
Tourism Board closed the park partially,

174
00:11:07,374 --> 00:11:10,027
for essential maintenance
and repair works,

175
00:11:10,107 --> 00:11:14,793
until further notice — saying the works
were needed to ensure public safety

176
00:11:14,873 --> 00:11:18,543
while preserving the park's
cultural and artistic elements,

177
00:11:18,623 --> 00:11:22,500
and that it was reviewing the
future plans for Haw Par Villa.

178
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:25,825
No reopening date has been announced.

179
00:11:25,825 --> 00:11:28,948
The park, unusually for a place so loved,

180
00:11:29,028 --> 00:11:32,075
is not gazetted as a national monument.

181
00:11:32,075 --> 00:11:36,024
What remains open is Hell's
Museum, run by an associate

182
00:11:36,104 --> 00:11:40,199
company of the former operator:
open daily from 10 in the

183
00:11:40,279 --> 00:11:44,374
morning to 6, at 22 Singapore
dollars for an adult and 12

184
00:11:44,454 --> 00:11:48,775
Singapore dollars for a child,
the Ten Courts at its heart.

185
00:11:48,775 --> 00:11:52,639
The Halloween event the
museum spawned, Haw Ror Villa,

186
00:11:52,719 --> 00:11:56,730
even returned in the autumn
of 2026 for a fifth edition,

187
00:11:56,810 --> 00:12:01,850
staged in and around the museum
while the grounds beyond stayed shut.

188
00:12:01,850 --> 00:12:06,010
The villa is gone, the ointment
empire is gone, and the garden

189
00:12:06,090 --> 00:12:10,249
has now outlived its makers, its
theme park and its managers —

190
00:12:10,329 --> 00:12:13,669
a hillside of painted
lessons, built to sell balm,

191
00:12:13,749 --> 00:12:18,125
waiting behind its fence for
Singapore to decide what it is for.
